@mnquintana, I *do* systematically disable as many third party trackers as possible, using both Ghostery and Noscript where possible.
I don't have a problem with Github knowing all of that, but I don't see why Google should. Why should Google even know which editor I'm using?

I appreciate that you're trying to inform about the tracking, but if possible I'd suggest linking to the package settings page (Settings>Packages>Metrics), where you could give the same information as on the web page, with the advantage of have the `Disable` button right there.

@uok disabling the package by default really isn't an option, like @batjko mentioned above:

> To be sure, disabling the package by default is the same as decommissioning it entirely. 
> Very few people will make an effort to enable it, even if asked, which makes collecting statistics on a tiny minority all but useless.

@Zireael07 That's a different question though. This issue was about the metrics package and the opt-out of that has been made clear. 
So the remaining question is whether this has to be made even clearer. 

If you're worried about exception-reporting to bugsnag, I'd recommend opening a new issue on that one (if there isn't one already).

@bronson, I was very careful in my wording:

> GitHub ... has a legal department that could advise or has already advised them

*Disclaimer: I am not an employee of GitHub nor a member of the Atom Core team. I'm just an enthusiastic volunteer.*

@bronson Please re-read what @lee-dohm wrote;

> [..] I'm certain that GitHub, on the other hand, has a legal department that could advise or has already advised them on what the actual boundaries are. So let's leave the law to the lawyers. [..]

He did not claim any such thing.

@vyp Well, since we're using a very useful product for free, and get constantly ongoing improvement being developed for it by the core devs, it's not wrong to care whether those devs get supported in their efforts, be it by allowing them to collect some data or otherwise.

As I mentioned above, personally I don't care about the data that the metrics package collects, as it's really minor stuff, and so I have no problem offering it up. So who cares? I kinda do.
But is it a problem if you don't? Not really. Especially if you contribute in other ways, no one can fault you for also offering constructive criticism. That's important, too.
